": \"The supplied review proposes this precursor entry in the context of Patel et al. (2015), but provides no independently verifiable substrate-specific experiment. Treat its geochemical operation as speculative. Conjugate addition is chemically reasonable, while competing carbonyl condensation, multiple additions and oligomerization make selective accumulation uncertain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Monoreductive amination of propanedial\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surfaces, such as awaruite; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 7-9; sulfur-poor microenvironment needed to reduce catalyst passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde condenses with ammonia, and hydrogenation of the resulting imine produces the amino aldehyde with net water release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. The supplied review mentions this precursor possibility without establishing a working prebiotic reaction. Nickel-bearing metal surfaces are chemically relevant to H2 activation, but their activity and selectivity here are unverified. Dialdehyde hydration, direct carbonyl reduction, diamination and polymerization compete; precursor availability is also unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated solution; approximately pH 7-9, allowing a reactive cyanide fraction while limiting amino-aldehyde decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and protonation gives the alpha-hydroxynitrile. Addition to either carbonyl face produces racemic material.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Attributed to Patel et al. (2015) by the supplied review; the elementary cyanohydrin chemistry is credible, although the exact experimental attribution has not been independently checked here. This is carbonyl cyanohydrin formation, not the complete ammonia-dependent Strecker reaction. Reversibility and amino-aldehyde instability limit net flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition and hydroxylated thiolactam formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K; exact effective range unverified\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-bearing pool; approximately pH 7-9; low accessible metal loading to limit sulfide precipitation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide addition to the nitrile generates thioimidate/thioamide functionality. Intramolecular attack by the terminal amine and ammonia elimination produce a five-membered cyclic thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review attributes this transformation to Patel et al. (2015). Five-membered ring closure is structurally plausible, but the attribution does not establish the proposed environmental rate or selectivity. Intermolecular reactions and slow ammonia displacement remain possible bottlenecks; no mineral catalyst is added without evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed photoreductive deoxygenation of the hydroxylated thiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes; substrate-specific activity hypothetical\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated anoxic shallow solution; sulfide is the sacrificial electron donor. Sulfur is represented as S8 for idealized net bookkeeping; actual sulfur speciation may include polysulfides.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hypothesized photoredox electron transfer enables C-O bond cleavage and protonation. Sulfide oxidation supplies the reducing equivalents, with net formation of water and elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific adaptation of the cyanosulfidic photoredox framework associated with Patel et al. (2015), not an independently verified correction of its experimental procedure. The review's equation omits two reducing equivalents. Including sulfide oxidation restores atom and electron bookkeeping. H2S does not generally justify assuming native copper formation from Cu2+; copper sulfide precipitation is a major competing sink.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed reductive cyanation of pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; hypothetical hydrogenation catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic mixing zone; approximately pH 6-8. Requires cyanide trapping before complete reduction and exceptional resistance to sulfur/cyanide poisoning.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The proposed net reaction combines partial reductive desulfurization with cyanide capture of an imine/iminium equivalent. H2 supplies the otherwise missing hydrogen equivalents; the product is racemic prolinonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and a major feasibility bottleneck. The supplied review attributes thione-to-nitrile conversion to Patel et al. (2015), but its equation is short by H2 and cannot be simple cyanide displacement as written. This balanced hydrogenative alternative is not established by that citation. Native alloy surfaces are considered only for H2 activation; sulfide poisoning, cyanide binding and overreduction to pyrrolidine may prevent useful conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed cyanohydrin deoxygenation before ring closure\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation under anoxic conditions; sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. S8 is an idealized terminal sulfur product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hypothesized reductive cleavage of the cyanohydrin C-O bond replaces hydroxyl with hydrogen while retaining the nitrile, giving 4-aminobutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; the supplied evidence does not demonstrate this substrate-specific transformation. Patel et al. (2015) supports the broader photoredox framework, not selective execution of this branch. It tests a chemically different order of deoxygenation and cyclization. Nitrile reduction, retrocyanohydrin fragmentation and catalyst precipitation may dominate instead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct thiolactam formation from 4-aminobutanenitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-360 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-bearing pool; approximately pH 7-9; thermal cycling may assist equilibration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile thiolysis followed by intramolecular aminolysis and ammonia elimination produces pyrrolidine-2-thione directly.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative extension of the nitrile/sulfide ring-forming chemistry attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the review. The amino-nitrile spacing permits a five-membered ring and avoids deoxygenation of an already sulfur-containing heterocycle. Actual rates and competition with open-chain thioamides are not established by the supplied evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial nitrile hydrogenation and intramolecular transimination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfur-poor interface; approximately pH 7-9. Product must escape or be trapped before further hydrogenation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Partial nitrile reduction gives an aldimine equivalent. The terminal amine undergoes intramolecular transimination, releasing ammonia and forming 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; no supporting experiment is provided in the review. The net reaction is balanced and five-membered cyclic imine formation is chemically reasonable. Selective partial hydrogenation is the principal weakness: full reduction to a diamine or subsequent pyrroline reduction can outcompete the desired branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Monoreductive amination of butanedial\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 7-9; low sulfide activity and immediate intramolecular trapping are required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condensation at one aldehyde followed by imine hydrogenation produces 4-aminobutanal and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Reductive amination supplies the correct four-carbon amino-aldehyde skeleton without the cyanosulfidic ring-building sequence. The supplied literature does not establish this prebiotic route or a sustained butanedial source. Dialdehyde polymerization, carbonyl hydrogenation and diamination are important competitors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclocondensation of 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic interface; approximately pH 6-9; reversible amino-aldehyde/cyclic-imine equilibrium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular amine addition to the aldehyde forms a cyclic hemiaminal, followed by water elimination to 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Standard five-membered imine-forming chemistry; its operation within this prebiotic sequence is speculative because it is not documented in the supplied evidence. Cyclization offers a chemically justified sink for an unstable amino aldehyde, but aqueous equilibrium and further hydrogenation limit accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type cyanation of a preformed cyclic imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanide-bearing pool; approximately pH 6-8; pyrroline must be captured faster than hydrolysis or oligomerization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon and proton transfer gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack from either face yields a racemate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chemically conventional imine cyanation, but speculative as a complete prebiotic route under the supplied evidence. The review's assertion that Strecker chemistry cannot yield cyclic amino acids is too broad: cyanation of a preformed cyclic imine avoids that limitation. No L-selectivity is implied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-assisted hydration of prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K; proposed accelerated-hydrolysis range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic acidic geothermal pool; approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence may be needed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation and water addition produce an imidic intermediate that tautomerizes to the primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review attributes overall aminonitrile hydrolysis to Patel et al. (2015). Resolving it into hydration and amide hydrolysis exposes a real kinetic bottleneck. The warmer acidic conditions are a proposed geochemical adaptation, not conditions verified by that citation; ambient neutral hydrolysis should not be assumed rapid.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic acidic pool; approximately pH 1-3. Ammonia is represented in neutral formula form although ammonium dominates here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition-elimination hydrolyzes the primary amide to the carboxylic acid and ammonia. Racemic feed gives equal L- and D-proline outputs; stoichiometric coefficients are implicit.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This resolves the second part of hydrolysis attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the review. Slow amide cleavage may determine overall residence time. Crucially, neither the provided achiral structures nor hydrolysis establishes L-selectivity. The target is obtained only as the L component of racemic proline, not as an enantiopure product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-keto-group reductive amination of 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; low sulfide activity. Regioselectivity is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condensation and hydrogenation at the alpha-keto group generate racemic glutamate 5-semialdehyde while retaining the terminal aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; no supplied paper demonstrates this selective prebiotic transformation. It provides a chemically recognizable entry to the pyrroline-5-carboxylate skeleton. However, aldehyde amination or reduction may dominate, so this is a possible competing product channel rather than a claimed selective synthesis. The C5 feedstock's prebiotic availability is an independent major gap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclocondensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; cyclic imine and open-chain aldehyde remain in equilibrium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha amino group attacks the terminal aldehyde, and dehydration closes the five-membered ring to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The ring-chain relationship is established chemical logic, but this prebiotic implementation is speculative and is not supported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Five-membered intramolecular condensation is more credible than invoking an uncatalyzed direct reduction of glutamate's carboxyl group. The existing stereocenter remains racemic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfur-poor microenvironment; approximately pH 6-9.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the cyclic imine produces the saturated pyrrolidine ring without requiring reduction of the carboxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a mineral-mediated prebiotic reaction; not demonstrated in the supplied literature. Imine hydrogenation is chemically more accessible than carboxylic-acid or lactam reduction, justifying examination of this branch. Catalyst availability, carboxylate adsorption and competing decomposition remain unquantified. Racemic precursor yields both proline enantiomers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Terminal-aldehyde reductive amination of 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; sulfur-poor interface; the alpha-keto group must survive.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of the terminal aldehyde produces 5-amino-2-oxopentanoic acid, retaining the alpha-keto acid functionality.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; the supplied literature does not establish this route. Aldehyde reductive amination is chemically plausible and leads to a different ring-closing topology from alpha-keto-group amination. Direct carbonyl hydrogenation, double amination and alpha-keto-acid decomposition compete. Availability of the starting ketoaldehyde acid remains unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclocondensation to pyrroline-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; reversible intramolecular condensation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine attacks the alpha-keto group; dehydration produces a five-membered cyclic imine whose imine carbon bears the carboxyl substituent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative prebiotic implementation of standard imine chemistry; no supporting experiment is supplied. This is constitutionally distinct from the pyrroline-5-carboxylate branch and uses the correct amine-carbonyl spacing for five-membered closure. Hydration, imine hydrolysis and keto-acid decomposition constrain accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of pyrroline-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfur-poor microenvironment; approximately pH 6-9; no chiral catalyst is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the planar imine carbon establishes the proline alpha stereocenter. Equivalent attack on either face gives L- and D-proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative under prebiotic mineral conditions; the supplied literature provides no demonstration. The required transformation is ordinary imine reduction rather than a difficult carboxyl reduction, but activity of native alloys toward this substrate is unverified. Achiral conditions cannot justify selective L-proline production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Paal-Knorr condensation to pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions for general acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrating pool with wet-dry cycling; mildly acidic wet phase. Two water equivalents are produced, without repeated output IDs.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with the 1,4-dicarbonyl unit, followed by cyclization and two dehydrations to the aromatic pyrrole ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction class is chemically established, but this specific prebiotic substrate-and-environment combination is speculative and unsupported by the supplied review. The ketoaldehyde has the required 1,4-dicarbonyl topology. Aromatization could provide a persistent intermediate during concentration; keto-acid decarboxylation, polymerization and uncertain feedstock supply are major limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid to proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; hypothetical aromatic-ring hydrogenation catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-450 K; proposed range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated hydrothermal pressure with substantial dissolved H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native nickel-iron alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfur-poor interface; stronger hydrogenating conditions than for imine reduction are likely required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Addition of two H2 equivalents per pyrrole ring saturates both carbon-carbon unsaturations, yielding racemic pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a geochemical reaction; the supplied literature does not establish it. Pyrrole-ring hydrogenation is chemically possible, but natural alloy performance cannot be inferred from laboratory hydrogenation catalysts. Aromatic stabilization, nitrogen binding, sulfur poisoning and decarboxylation may make this branch ineffective. It is a lower-confidence alternative, not evidence of an additional demonstrated prebiotic route.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein entry through the hydroxylated-thiolactam sequence\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein supplies 3-aminopropanal, followed by cyanohydrin formation, sulfur-assisted ring closure, proposed deoxygenation and reductive cyanation, then hydrolysis. Closest to the supplied review's framework, but corrected redox steps remain unverified. Produces L-proline with D-proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Propanedial entry through the hydroxylated-thiolactam sequence\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative monoreductive amination replaces acrolein conjugate addition as the precursor entry. The subsequent thiolactam route shares P1's redox and selectivity bottlenecks and ends in racemic proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein entry with deoxygenation before thiolactam closure\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative order-of-operations alternative: remove the cyanohydrin hydroxyl before sulfide-assisted ring formation. This is chemically distinct from deoxygenating the cyclic thioamide, but selective cyanohydrin deoxygenation is unestablished.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Propanedial entry with deoxygenation before thiolactam closure\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines speculative propanedial monoreductive amination with early cyanohydrin deoxygenation and direct thiolactam formation. It is a distinct complete reaction sequence, not an independently demonstrated synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein entry through nitrile reduction and cyclic-imine cyanation\",\n      \"description\": \"After proposed cyanohydrin deoxygenation, partial nitrile hydrogenation and transimination form pyrroline without a thiolactam intermediate. Cyclic-imine cyanation and hydrolysis yield both proline enantiomers. Avoiding overreduction is a major unresolved requirement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Propanedial entry through nitrile reduction and cyclic-imine cyanation\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative propanedial reductive amination feeds the partial-nitrile-reduction branch. This route bypasses sulfur incorporation into the ring, although sulfide is still consumed in the proposed upstream photodeoxygenation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Butanedial reductive amination and cyclic Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"A sulfur-independent ring-building candidate: butanedial gives 4-aminobutanal, which cyclizes to pyrroline before cyanide addition and hydrolysis. Feedstock availability and selective monoamination are unresolved; the final product is racemic.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketoaldehyde acid through glutamate semialdehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional on a supply of 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid, alpha-keto reductive amination gives racemic glutamate semialdehyde. Cyclocondensation and imine hydrogenation produce L- and D-proline without cyanide. Regioselectivity and mineral-catalyzed reduction are speculative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_024\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketoaldehyde acid through 5-amino-2-oxopentanoic acid\",\n      \"description\": \"The alternative reductive-amination regiochemistry generates a terminal amine, followed by pyrroline-2-carboxylate formation and reduction. This is distinct from the glutamate-semialdehyde route, but remains a conditional, unverified prebiotic pathway.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_025\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Paal-Knorr aromatization followed by pyrrole hydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"The same conditional ketoaldehyde-acid feedstock undergoes ammonia-driven aromatic ring formation, then hydrogenation in a separate H2-rich setting. This lower-confidence pathway tests an aromatic storage intermediate; no effective natural catalyst or overall prebiotic synthesis is established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_026\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_021\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "This graph enumerates ten distinct candidate reaction-sequence pathways, not ten experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. Six combine two precursor-entry reactions with three alternative ring-building sequences; four explore cyclic-imine and pyrrole routes. The supplied review attributes a cyanosulfidic route to Patel et al. (2015), but its deoxygenation and thione-to-aminonitrile equations omit reducing equivalents, and its structures do not establish L-selectivity. Those problems are corrected or explicitly identified below. References to that paper distinguish review-level attribution from substrate-specific evidence. Unsupported extensions are marked speculative. All achiral syntheses produce L-proline together with D-proline; no demonstrated prebiotic enantiomeric resolution is assumed. Organic feedstocks are explicit boundary inputs: especially propanedial, butanedial and 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid have unresolved prebiotic supply and concentration. Thus, this is a complete specification of a conditional reaction graph, not a demonstrated continuous synthesis from C1 feedstocks. Proposed conditions are not experimentally verified optima. Incompatible photochemical, hydrogenation and hydrolysis stages require separation, transport or environmental cycling. Null formation environments indicate imported species or formation in multiple settings. The schema's intermediate role also accommodates unavoidable byproducts.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null