 "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C5H7NO2", "iupac_name": "4,5-Dihydro-3H-pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "Delta-1-pyrroline-2-carboxylic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C5H5NO2", "iupac_name": "1H-Pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "Pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Ammonia conjugate addition to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic pool; approximately pH 8-10; rapid downstream trapping would be required to limit secondary reactions."}, "mechanism": "Aza-Michael addition of ammonia at the beta carbon of acrolein, followed by proton transfer, gives 3-aminopropanal.", "reasoning": "The supplied review proposes this precursor entry in the context of Patel et al. (2015), but provides no independently verifiable substrate-specific experiment. Treat its geochemical operation as speculative. Conjugate addition is chemically reasonable, while competing carbonyl condensation, multiple additions and oligomerization make selective accumulation uncertain."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Monoreductive amination of propanedial", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surfaces, such as awaruite; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 7-9; sulfur-poor microenvironment needed to reduce catalyst passivation."}, "mechanism": "One aldehyde condenses with ammonia, and hydrogenation of the resulting imine produces the amino aldehyde with net water release.", "reasoning": "Speculative. The supplied review mentions this precursor possibility without establishing a working prebiotic reaction. Nickel-bearing metal surfaces are chemically relevant to H2 activation, but their activity and selectivity here are unverified. Dialdehyde hydration, direct carbonyl reduction, diamination and polymerization compete; precursor availability is also unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation from 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated solution; approximately pH 7-9, allowing a reactive cyanide fraction while limiting amino-aldehyde decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and protonation gives the alpha-hydroxynitrile. Addition to either carbonyl face produces racemic material.", "reasoning": "Attributed to Patel et al. (2015) by the supplied review; the elementary cyanohydrin chemistry is credible, although the exact experimental attribution has not been independently checked here. This is carbonyl cyanohydrin formation, not the complete ammonia-dependent Strecker reaction. Reversibility and amino-aldehyde instability limit net flux."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Sulfide addition and hydroxylated thiolactam formation", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K; exact effective range unverified", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-bearing pool; approximately pH 7-9; low accessible metal loading to limit sulfide precipitation."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide addition to the nitrile generates thioimidate/thioamide functionality. Intramolecular attack by the terminal amine and ammonia elimination produce a five-membered cyclic thioamide.", "reasoning": "The supplied review attributes this transformation to Patel et al. (2015). Five-membered ring closure is structurally plausible, but the attribution does not establish the proposed environmental rate or selectivity. Intermolecular reactions and slow ammonia displacement remain possible bottlenecks; no mineral catalyst is added without evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Proposed photoreductive deoxygenation of the hydroxylated thiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001", "mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes; substrate-specific activity hypothetical"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated anoxic shallow solution; sulfide is the sacrificial electron donor. Sulfur is represented as S8 for idealized net bookkeeping; actual sulfur speciation may include polysulfides."}, "mechanism": "Hypothesized photoredox electron transfer enables C-O bond cleavage and protonation. Sulfide oxidation supplies the reducing equivalents, with net formation of water and elemental sulfur.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific adaptation of the cyanosulfidic photoredox framework associated with Patel et al. (2015), not an independently verified correction of its experimental procedure. The review's equation omits two reducing equivalents. Including sulfide oxidation restores atom and electron bookkeeping. H2S does not generally justify assuming native copper formation from Cu2+; copper sulfide precipitation is a major competing sink."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Proposed reductive cyanation of pyrrolidine-2-thione", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; hypothetical hydrogenation catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic mixing zone; approximately pH 6-8. Requires cyanide trapping before complete reduction and exceptional resistance to sulfur/cyanide poisoning."}, "mechanism": "The proposed net reaction combines partial reductive desulfurization with cyanide capture of an imine/iminium equivalent. H2 supplies the otherwise missing hydrogen equivalents; the product is racemic prolinonitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative and a major feasibility bottleneck. The supplied review attributes thione-to-nitrile conversion to Patel et al. (2015), but its equation is short by H2 and cannot be simple cyanide displacement as written. This balanced hydrogenative alternative is not established by that citation. Native alloy surfaces are considered only for H2 activation; sulfide poisoning, cyanide binding and overreduction to pyrrolidine may prevent useful conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Proposed cyanohydrin deoxygenation before ring closure", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001", "mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation under anoxic conditions; sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. S8 is an idealized terminal sulfur product."}, "mechanism": "Hypothesized reductive cleavage of the cyanohydrin C-O bond replaces hydroxyl with hydrogen while retaining the nitrile, giving 4-aminobutanenitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative; the supplied evidence does not demonstrate this substrate-specific transformation. Patel et al. (2015) supports the broader photoredox framework, not selective execution of this branch. It tests a chemically different order of deoxygenation and cyclization. Nitrile reduction, retrocyanohydrin fragmentation and catalyst precipitation may dominate instead."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Direct thiolactam formation from 4-aminobutanenitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-360 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-bearing pool; approximately pH 7-9; thermal cycling may assist equilibration."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile thiolysis followed by intramolecular aminolysis and ammonia elimination produces pyrrolidine-2-thione directly.", "reasoning": "Speculative extension of the nitrile/sulfide ring-forming chemistry attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the review. The amino-nitrile spacing permits a five-membered ring and avoids deoxygenation of an already sulfur-containing heterocycle. Actual rates and competition with open-chain thioamides are not established by the supplied evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Partial nitrile hydrogenation and intramolecular transimination", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfur-poor interface; approximately pH 7-9. Product must escape or be trapped before further hydrogenation."}, "mechanism": "Partial nitrile reduction gives an aldimine equivalent. The terminal amine undergoes intramolecular transimination, releasing ammonia and forming 1-pyr