ence is speculative because it is not documented in the supplied evidence. Cyclization offers a chemically justified sink for an unstable amino aldehyde, but aqueous equilibrium and further hydrogenation limit accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Strecker-type cyanation of a preformed cyclic imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic cyanide-bearing pool; approximately pH 6-8; pyrroline must be captured faster than hydrolysis or oligomerization."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the cyclic imine/iminium carbon and proton transfer gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. Attack from either face yields a racemate.", "reasoning": "Chemically conventional imine cyanation, but speculative as a complete prebiotic route under the supplied evidence. The review's assertion that Strecker chemistry cannot yield cyclic amino acids is too broad: cyanation of a preformed cyclic imine avoids that limitation. No L-selectivity is implied."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Acid-assisted hydration of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K; proposed accelerated-hydrolysis range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic acidic geothermal pool; approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence may be needed."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation and water addition produce an imidic intermediate that tautomerizes to the primary amide.", "reasoning": "The supplied review attributes overall aminonitrile hydrolysis to Patel et al. (2015). Resolving it into hydration and amide hydrolysis exposes a real kinetic bottleneck. The warmer acidic conditions are a proposed geochemical adaptation, not conditions verified by that citation; ambient neutral hydrolysis should not be assumed rapid."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Prolinamide hydrolysis to racemic proline", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic acidic pool; approximately pH 1-3. Ammonia is represented in neutral formula form although ammonium dominates here."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition-elimination hydrolyzes the primary amide to the carboxylic acid and ammonia. Racemic feed gives equal L- and D-proline outputs; stoichiometric coefficients are implicit.", "reasoning": "This resolves the second part of hydrolysis attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the review. Slow amide cleavage may determine overall residence time. Crucially, neither the provided achiral structures nor hydrolysis establishes L-selectivity. The target is obtained only as the L component of racemic proline, not as an enantiopure product."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Alpha-keto-group reductive amination of 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; low sulfide activity. Regioselectivity is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condensation and hydrogenation at the alpha-keto group generate racemic glutamate 5-semialdehyde while retaining the terminal aldehyde.", "reasoning": "Speculative; no supplied paper demonstrates this selective prebiotic transformation. It provides a chemically recognizable entry to the pyrroline-5-carboxylate skeleton. However, aldehyde amination or reduction may dominate, so this is a possible competing product channel rather than a claimed selective synthesis. The C5 feedstock's prebiotic availability is an independent major gap."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclocondensation", "inputs": ["mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; cyclic imine and open-chain aldehyde remain in equilibrium."}, "mechanism": "The alpha amino group attacks the terminal aldehyde, and dehydration closes the five-membered ring to pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid.", "reasoning": "The ring-chain relationship is established chemical logic, but this prebiotic implementation is speculative and is not supported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Five-membered intramolecular condensation is more credible than invoking an uncatalyzed direct reduction of glutamate's carboxyl group. The existing stereocenter remains racemic."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Hydrogenation of pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfur-poor microenvironment; approximately pH 6-9."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the cyclic imine produces the saturated pyrrolidine ring without requiring reduction of the carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "Speculative as a mineral-mediated prebiotic reaction; not demonstrated in the supplied literature. Imine hydrogenation is chemically more accessible than carboxylic-acid or lactam reduction, justifying examination of this branch. Catalyst availability, carboxylate adsorption and competing decomposition remain unquantified. Racemic precursor yields both proline enantiomers."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Terminal-aldehyde reductive amination of 2,5-dioxopentanoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible native nickel-iron alloy surface; proposed catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K; proposed range", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure with dissolved H2", "catalyst": "Native nickel-iron alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; sulfur-poor interface; the alpha-keto group must survive."}, "mechanism": "Reductive amination of the terminal aldehyde produces 5-amino-2-oxopentanoic acid, retaining the alpha-keto acid functionality.", "reasoning": "Speculative; the supplied literature does not establish this route. Aldehyde reductive amination is chemically plausible and leads to a different ring-closing topology from alpha-keto-group amination. Direct carbonyl hydrogenation, double amination and alpha-keto-acid decomposition compete. Availability of the starting ketoaldehyde acid remains unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Cyclocondensation to pyrroline-2-carboxylic acid", "inputs": ["mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Elevated hydrothermal pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; reversible intramolecular condensation."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amine attacks the alpha-keto group; dehydration produces a five-membered cyclic imine whose imine carbon bears the carboxyl substituent.", "reasoning": "Speculative prebiotic implementation of standard imine chemistry; no supporting experiment is supplied. This is constitutionally distinct from the py