stribution.\",\n        \"Show that pyrroline survives transfer to cyanation conditions and establish subsequent nitrile and amide hydrolysis rates.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.35,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.48,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.57,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.73,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.45,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.54,\n      \"feedback\": \"The cyclic-imine cyanation and hydrolysis segment is chemically credible. Upstream, selective propanedial monoamination, cyanohydrin deoxygenation and partial nitrile hydrogenation are not demonstrated under specified conditions. In particular, H2 availability alone does not establish selective production and preservation of pyrroline. The graph reaches racemic proline formally, but actual production depends on resolving these upstream selectivity and transfer problems.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure monoamination selectivity and reactive intermediate lifetimes starting from the stated propanedial input.\",\n        \"Validate rxn_007 and selective imine production in rxn_009 with explicit catalysts and product balances.\",\n        \"Specify conditions that preserve pyrroline during transfer and complete both hydrolysis stages.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.6,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.57,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.69,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.71,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a credible conditional route: 4-aminobutanal cyclization, cyclic-imine cyanation and aminonitrile hydrolysis are sound transformations. The main bottleneck is selective formation of the aminoaldehyde and preservation of pyrroline during rxn_010\u2013rxn_011, since continued hydrogenation can produce pyrrolidine instead. Competing dialdehyde chemistry and pyrroline loss during transfer also require evaluation. No demonstrated catalyst conditions or integrated yields are supplied. Racemic production nevertheless includes the requested L enantiomer.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate productive butanedial monoamination and cyclization while quantifying pyrrolidine, direct carbonyl-reduction products and condensation products.\",\n        \"Specify how pyrroline leaves hydrogenating conditions and is trapped by cyanide before hydrolysis or oligomerization.\",\n        \"Provide substrate-specific evidence and realistic pH, temperature and residence times for complete conversion to proline.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.58,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.73,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.57,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.68,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.7,\n      \"feedback\": \"Glutamate-semialdehyde cyclization and subsequent imine reduction constitute a chemically coherent route. The weak point is selective alpha-keto reductive amination of a substrate also containing an aldehyde; catalyst identity, competing carbonyl reduction and substrate stability are unspecified. Terminal-aldehyde amination is not necessarily a network-level loss because P9 consumes that product. Hydrogenation of racemic pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid would preserve access to both proline enantiomers, but the proposed environmental implementation remains unverified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Determine the product distribution of ketoaldehyde reductive amination, including both network-recognized regioisomers and genuinely unproductive products.\",\n        \"Specify a realistic catalyst, its mineral or metallic state, H2 fugacity, pH and temperature for the reductive steps.\",\n        \"Measure in situ cyclization and proline formation, including ketoaldehyde stability and possible decarboxylation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.66,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.77,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.72,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"Accepting the stated ketoaldehyde boundary input, this is the strongest conditional pathway. Terminal-aldehyde reductive amination, five-membered imine formation and imine hydrogenation are mechanistically credible, and continued imine reduction here gives the desired product rather than diverting it. Nevertheless, selective amination and productive H2 activation by an environmentally available catalyst have not been demonstrated. Competing carbonyl reduction, further amination and substrate decomposition remain unresolved. The expected racemate contains L-proline but establishes no enantiomeric enrichment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate rxn_018\u2013rxn_020 with the named substrate and quantify flux into proline versus competing products.\",\n        \"Document catalyst availability and activity under specified temperature, pH, H2 fugacity and water composition.\",\n        \"Provide primary experimental references or explicitly retain this branch as an unverified proposal, and measure product identity, yield and enantiomeric composition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.48\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.63,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.51,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.53,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.61,\n      \"feedback\": \"The ketoaldehyde has the required 1,4-dicarbonyl arrangement for a Paal\u2013Knorr condensation, and rxn_021 correctly releases two waters. Pyrrole saturation is also balanced at two H2 per substrate, but aromatic-ring hydrogenation requires a suitable active catalyst and is more demanding than the imine reductions in P8 and P9. No supporting catalyst, pressure or substrate-specific performance is supplied. Decarboxylation and other decomposition during processing must be assessed. Successful hydrogenation would produce racemic proline.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_022\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Verify pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid formation and retention of its carboxyl group under the proposed condensation conditions.\",\n        \"Demonstrate rxn_022 with an environmentally realistic catalyst and explicit H2 fugacity, temperature and reaction time.\",\n        \"Measure intermediate survival during transfer and distinguish proline formation from decarboxylation or ring-degradation products.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.64,\n  \"network_feedback\