ting data for isolated prolinamide. The stated implementation is therefore speculative. This is a conventional amide-to-acid transformation, and sufficiently persistent acidity and heating are more credible than assuming rapid neutral hydrolysis. Both enantiomer nodes are genuine products of the unresolved precursor pool; balancing infers the corresponding coefficients rather than interpreting one precursor molecule as producing two proline molecules.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline prolinamide hydrolysis to an L/D-proline mixture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide-bearing alkaline geothermal water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide-mediated hydrolysis; alkalinity is also consumed in carboxylate formation\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: prolinamide 0.1-20 mM, analytical hydroxide concentration 0.05-0.5 M, and residence time 1-21 days. The alkaline reservoir must supply at least the acid-equivalent capacity required by conversion, not merely an initial pH reading. Listed proline formulas represent the parent compounds; prolinate dominates under strongly alkaline conditions. Quantify decomposition and racemization rather than assuming complete conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl. Tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and proton transfer yield prolinate and ammonia; environmental proton exchange determines the observed proline protonation state.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative environmental implementation of standard alkaline amide hydrolysis. No prolinamide-specific alkaline kinetics or yields are provided in the supplied research, and Patel et al. (2015) is not treated as establishing these conditions. This option avoids a concentrated acidic compartment but requires unusually persistent alkalinity and sufficient residence time. Racemization would not eliminate the L component, although degradation would reduce total yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional sulfide addition to prolinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar with a confined sulfide-containing headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: prolinonitrile 0.1-10 mM, residual dissolved total sulfide 10-100 mM after metal-sulfide precipitation, pH 8.0-9.5, and residence time 6 hours to 7 days. Use a separate compartment from the Ni-Fe hydrogenation surface. Assess sulfide availability after reaction with abundant Fe2+, rather than assuming the full delivered sulfide remains dissolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon; proton transfers produce the primary thioamide without changing the pyrrolidine carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate extension. The supplied review associates nitrile thiolation with Patel et al. (2015), but its example is an open-chain aminocyanohydrin, not prolinonitrile. It therefore does not validate this reaction's rate or selectivity. This sulfur-assisted branch involves addition and hydrolysis rather than the review's problematic reductive thione-to-nitrile exchange. FeS precipitation, H2S escape, and aminonitrile decomposition are explicit potential losses.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional hydrolytic conversion of prolinethioamide to prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic geothermal water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Proton-mediated thioamide hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: thioamide 0.1-10 mM, analytical acid concentration 0.01-0.5 M, and residence time 12 hours to 14 days. Transition from the sulfide-rich formation compartment to an acidic compartment is required. Measure amide, acid, residual thioamide, and sulfur-containing side products; H2S release does not imply quantitative environmental recycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the activated thioamide and proton transfer permit replacement of sulfur by oxygen, giving prolinamide and hydrogen sulfide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative: no substrate-specific primary support is supplied for this hydrolysis. Thioamide-to-amide conversion is chemically reasonable, but persistence of the thioamide, alternative cleavage, and direct progression to the acid must be tested. This step is the principal bottleneck of the sulfur-assisted workups. It requires no molecular oxygen and no assumed native-copper formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Exploratory carboxylative conversion of prolinonitrile to a bicyclic hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ammonia/ammonium acid-base buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar with a CO2-containing headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid/base assistance by the aqueous buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: prolinonitrile 0.1-10 mM, total dissolved inorganic carbon 0.05-0.5 M, total ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M, measured pH 8.0-9.5, and residence time 1-14 days. Carbon supply, alkalinity, and CO2 exchange must be mutually compatible. The CO2 node represents the incorporated inorganic-carbon equivalent, not an assumption that all dissolved carbon is molecular CO2.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed N-carboxylation of the secondary aminonitrile followed by intramolecular nitrile capture and rearrangement produces a fused hydantoin. The nitrile nitrogen becomes the second hydantoin nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Explicitly speculative and lower-confidence than direct hydrolysis. General hydantoin chemistry provides an analogy, as reviewed by Ware (1950), but neither that general analogy nor the supplied Patel et al. (2015) account establishes successful Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of this secondary cyclic aminonitrile under prebiotic conditions. Formation of the proposed bicyclic product must be demonstrated rather than inferred from atom balance. Competing reversible carbamate formation may prevent useful conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Exploratory opening of the bicyclic hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide-bearing geothermal water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide-mediated ring-opening hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: hydantoin 0.1-10 mM, analytical hydroxide concentration 0.01-0.2 M, and residence time 12 hours to 14 days. Verify which hydantoin C-N bond cleaves and whether N-carbamoylproline accumulates. Acid/base equivalents associated with product carboxylate formation must be supplied by the local reservoir.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the hydantoin amide bond adjacent to the proline-derived carbonyl opens the imidazolidinedione ring while preserving the pyrrolidine ring, producing N-carbamoylproline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin opening to N-carbamoyl amino acids has general chemical precedent discussed by Ware (1950). The specific fused substrate, opening selectivity, and environmental rate are not established by the supplied research, so this implementation is speculative. General hydantoin precedent is not treated as reaction-specific primary evidence for this proposed proline branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Exploratory decarbamoylation to an L/D-proline mixture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic aqueous reservoir supplied by volcanic HCl\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Proton-mediated urea hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: N-carbamoylproline 0.1-10 mM, analytical HCl concentration 0.5-2 M, and residence time 1-21 days. An acidic compartment is required after alkaline ring opening. Measure carbamoyl-group removal independently of substrate disappearance; allow for CO2 escape and ammonia protonation. No enzyme or nitrosating reagent is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis removes the N-carbamoyl group through carbamic-acid-equivalent intermediates, ultimately releasing carbon dioxide and ammonia and restoring the secondary amine of proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative: the supplied research does not establish chemical decarbamoylation of N-carbamoylproline under these conditions. Urea-bond hydrolysis is a plausible endpoint but may be too slow or require acidity incompatible with a persistent natural pool. This is an additional independent bottleneck of the hydantoin branch, not a demonstrated completion step. Achiral processing produces both L- and D-proline; no claim of enantiopure L-proline is made.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde entry with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Accept freshly available 4-aminobutanal as an explicit boundary input, cyclize, cyanate, and perform sequential acidic nitrile and amide hydrolysis. This is the most defensible conditional core, but substrate-specific kinetics, environmental acid availability, and integrated yield remain unverified. Produces an L-containing racemate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde entry with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the same ring-forming and cyanating chemistry as P1, followed by an alkaline hydrolysis regime. This is a distinct processing sequence, not a separately established carbon-skeleton synthesis. Requires persistent alkalinity and aminonitrile survival.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and acidic completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Divert prolinonitrile through a proposed thioamide, hydrolyze that intermediate to prolinamide, and finish under acidic conditions. Thioamide formation and hydrolysis are unverified substrate-specific extensions; sulfur recycling is not assumed quantitative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and alkaline completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Follow the proposed sulfur-assisted route to prolinamide, then transfer to an alkaline compartment for the final hydrolysis. Both thioamide processing and the required acid-to-base environmental transition remain conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde entry with exploratory hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Propose carboxylative capture of prolinonitrile as a bicyclic hydantoin, followed by ring opening and decarbamoylation. This is a lower-confidence hypothesis with multiple unverified substrate-specific transformations, not a reported prebiotic proline synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde entry with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepend conditional reductive monoamination of succinaldehyde to the P1 sequence. Requires demonstrated formation and rapid release of 4-aminobutanal before overreduction, further amination, or condensation. No integrated yield is available.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde entry with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine the conditional hydrothermal precursor-supply branch with cyclic-imine cyanation and alkaline hydrolysis. The hydrogenation surface must be excluded from the cyanation compartment, and short-lived precursor delivery must be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and acidic completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine conditional dialdehyde monoamination with sulfur-assisted aminonitrile processing and acidic completion. Both precursor selectivity and thioamide hydrolysis are unresolved bottlenecks. Sulfide and cyanide must remain separated from the upstream hydrogenation surface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and alkaline completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the dialdehyde precursor branch, proposed sulfur-assisted hydration, and an alkaline final hydrolysis. This sequence adds demanding catalyst-separation and pH-transition requirements to already unverified upstream and sulfur-processing reactions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Dialdehyde entry with exploratory hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Connect conditional reductive monoamination to the exploratory hydantoin branch. This is the least-established complete sequence: precursor generation, bicyclic hydantoin formation, selective ring opening, and decarbamoylation all require direct testing. It is included as a transparent hypothesis, not as evidence that ten validated prebiotic routes exist.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "Construct the pyrrolidine skeleton by cyclization of 4-aminobutanal, capture the resulting cyclic imine with cyanide, and hydrolyze the aminonitrile. Include butane-1,4-dial reductive monoamination as a less-certain alternative precursor supply. Ten distinct reaction sequences are enumerated: two precursor-entry options combined with five downstream processing options. These are conditional network pathways, not ten experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. The most defensible core is aminoaldehyde cyclization, cyclic-imine cyanation, and acidic hydrolysis; the upstream hydrogenation, sulfur-assisted processing, and hydantoin branch require additional experimental validation. The supplied review does not establish L-selectivity: all target-producing reactions therefore include both L- and D-proline. Its proposed thione-to-aminonitrile exchange is not adopted because its stated equation omits reducing equivalents and does not establish selective reductive cyanation. Sulfide precipitation also does not establish production of native copper. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not verified reaction-specific procedures, yields, or residence times. Organic feedstocks are explicit boundary inputs; their environmental availability is not demonstrated. Neutral-parent formulas represent analytical compound pools, with pH-dependent protonation handled by the stated environmental acid/base reservoirs. No molecular oxygen, enzyme, unverified chiral resolution, or assumed metal-mediated enantioselection is used.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C4H6O2", "iupac_name": "butane-1,4-dial", "common_name": "Succinaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C4H9NO", "iupac_name": "4-aminobutanal", "common_name": "Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H7N", "iupac_name": "3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole", "common_name": "1-Pyrroline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrocyanic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": "Prolinonitrile, unresolved enantiomeric pool", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8N2/c6-4-5-2-1-3-7-5/h5,7H,1-3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H10N2O", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide", "common_name": "Prolinamide, unresolved enantiomeric pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H10N2S", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbothioamide", "common_name": "Prolinethioamide, unresolved enantiomeric pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "1,3-diazabicyclo[3.3.0]octane-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Proline-derived bicyclic hydantoin, unresolved enantiomeric pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C6H10N2O3", "iupac_name": "1-carbamoylpyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylproline, unresolved enantiomeric pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "D-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Conditional reductive monoamination of succinaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a candidate hydrogen-activation surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-380 K", "pressure": "50-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Awaruite; substrate-specific activity and selectivity unverified", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: succinaldehyde 0.1-1 mM, total ammonia/ammonium 50-500 mM, pH 7.5-9.0 measured at reaction temperature, and catalyst-contact times of 1-60 minutes. Exclude cyanide and sulfide from this compartment because they can inhibit or alter the metal surface. Product release, immediate cooling, and capture must outcompete further hydrogenation and diamination."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condenses with one aldehyde group; hydrogenation of the resulting imine gives 4-aminobutanal and water. The remaining aldehyde is retained for subsequent intramolecular imine formation.", "reasoning": "Speculative precursor-supply branch: no substrate-specific primary evidence for this awaruite reaction is supplied. A Ni-Fe surface is proposed for H2 activation, not as proof of selective monoamination. Carbonyl reduction, diamination, pyrrole formation, and hydrogenation of subsequently formed pyrroline to pyrrolidine are serious competitors. Specifying a screening window does not establish productive flux. This reaction is deliberately bypassed by pathways that accept 4-aminobutanal as an explicit boundary input; Patel et al. (2015) is not credited with demonstrating this hydrogenation."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Five-membered cyclic-imine formation", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: freshly supplied 4-aminobutanal 0.1-10 mM, pH 7.0-8.5, and residence times of 1 minute to 6 hours. Use short local transfer rather than long-distance ocean transport. Couple directly to cyanide capture; do not assume accumulation of a concentrated, indefinitely stable pyrroline stock. Shaded or optically screened water limits photochemical destruction."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amine attacks the aldehyde intramolecularly, forming a cyclic hemiaminal that loses water to give the five-membered imine 1-pyrroline.", "reasoning": "Five-membered aminoaldehyde cyclization is chemically well founded, but this environmental implementation and its quantitative equilibrium are speculative because no reaction-specific primary study is supplied. Aldehyde hydration, intermolecular condensation, imine hydrolysis, and pyrroline oligomerization must be measured. This uses a C4 aminoaldehyde, not the C3 substrate in the supplied Patel et al. (2015) narrative, and avoids the unsupported thione-reduction sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyclic-imine cyanation to prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a locally confined aqueous compartment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pyrroline 0.1-10 mM, total HCN/CN- 5-100 mM, pH 7.0-8.5, and sampling over 10 minutes to 48 hours. Maintain a confined headspace to limit HCN loss. Use a compartment physically separated from hydrogenation catalysts and rich dissolved transition metals. Determine cyanide capture versus imine hydrolysis and oligomerization experimentally."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the electrophilic carbon of the cyclic imine or its iminium form. Proton transfer yields pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile and creates the proline stereocenter without asymmetric control.", "reasoning": "This is the cyclic-imine extension of Strecker aminonitrile chemistry; Strecker (1850) supports the general reaction principle, not the specific environmental conditions proposed here. The supplied research provides no direct primary validation of this pyrroline capture experiment, so the prebiotic implementation is speculative. Contrary to the review's categorical statement, cyclic imines can provide cyclic amino-acid skeletons through Strecker-type cyanation. An achiral medium predicts an unresolved R/S aminonitrile pool, not L-selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Acid-promoted hydration of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic aqueous reservoir supplied by volcanic HCl"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Proton catalysis; acid reservoir also controls amine protonation", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aminonitrile 0.1-20 mM, analytical HCl concentration 0.1-1 M, and residence time 12 hours to 14 days. Reference pH at 298 K is approximately 0-1. Free cyanide should be depleted or separated before acidification. Monitor both amide accumulation and subsequent acid formation; an isolable amide plateau is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the primary carboxamide.", "reasoning": "The supplied review attributes prolinonitrile hydrolysis to Patel et al. (2015), but does not establish isolated-amide yields, acid concentrations, or substrate-specific kinetics. Accordingly, this operating window is speculative rather than a reported procedure. Heating and appreciable acidity address the review's ambient-neutral hydrolysis bottleneck. Retro-Strecker loss, substrate decomposition, and the availability of a sustained concentrated acid reservoir remain constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Alkaline hydration of prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide-bearing alkaline geothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-mediated hydration", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aminonitrile 0.1-20 mM, analytical hydroxide concentration 0.01-0.1 M, and residence time 1-21 days. These hydroxide concentrations correspond approximately to pH 12-13 at 298 K; hot-solution pH must be measured separately. Maintain water inventory against evaporation and quantify aminonitrile reversion and decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide adds to the nitrile carbon; proton exchange with water produces an imidate-derived intermediate that tautomerizes to prolinamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative alkaline alternative: the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review-level account does not document this substrate-specific alkaline implementation. Nitrile hydration is chemically credible, but the required exposure may exceed aminoaldehyde-derived aminonitrile stability. This branch is a different hydrolytic regime, not evidence for an additional independently demonstrated carbon-skeleton synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acidic prolinamide hydrolysis to an L/D-proline mixture", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic aqueous reservoir supplied by volcanic HCl"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Proton catalysis with a maintained acid reservoir", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: prolinamide 0.1-20 mM, analytical HCl concentration 0.5-2 M, and residence time 1-14 days. Sample over time for remaining amide, proline, and degradation products. Products exist predominantly as protonated proline and ammonium in this compartment; listed formulas are neutral-parent bookkeeping forms. No chiral enrichment is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleaves the exocyclic C-N bond, generating the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium while retaining the pyrrolidine ring.", "reasoning": "The supplied report's nitrile-hydrolysis endpoint is attributed to Patel et al. (2015), but it supplies no reaction-specific operating data for isolated prolinamide. The stated implementation is therefore speculative. This is a conventional amide-to-acid transformation, and sufficiently persistent acidity and heating are more credible than assuming rapid neutral hydrolysis. Both enantiomer nodes are genuine products of the unresolved precursor pool; balancing infers the corresponding coefficients rather than interpreting one precursor molecule as producing two proline molecules."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Alkaline prolinamide hydrolysis to an L/D-proline mixture", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide-bearing alkaline geothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-mediated hydrolysis; alkalinity is also consumed in carboxylate formation", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: prolinamide 0.1-20 mM, analytical hydroxide concentration 0.05-0.5 M, and residence time 1-21 days. The alkaline reservoir must supply at least the acid-equivalent capacity required by conversion, not merely an initial pH reading. Listed proline formulas represent the parent compounds; prolinate dominates under strongly alkaline conditions. Quantify decomposition and racemization rather than assuming complete conversion."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl. Tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and proton transfer yield prolinate and ammonia; environmental proton exchange determines the observed proline protonation state.", "reasoning": "Speculative environmental implementation of standard alkaline amide hydrolysis. No prolinamide-specific alkaline kinetics or yields are provided in the supplied research, and Patel et al. (2015) is not treated as establishing these conditions. This option avoids a concentrated acidic compartment but requires unusually persistent alkalinity and sufficient residence time. Racemization would not eliminate the L component, although degradation would reduce total yield."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Conditional sulfide addition to prolinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar with a confined sulfide-containing headspace", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: prolinonitrile 0.1-10 mM, residual dissolved total sulfide 10-100 mM after metal-sulfide precipitation, pH 8.0-9.5, and residence time 6 hours to 7 days. Use a separate compartment from the Ni-Fe hydrogenation surface. Assess sulfide availability after reaction with abundant Fe2+, rather than assuming the full delivered sulfide remains dissolved."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon; proton transfers produce the primary thioamide without changing the pyrrolidine carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate extension. The supplied review associates nitrile thiolation with Patel et al. (2015), but its example is an open-chain aminocyanohydrin, not prolinonitrile. It therefore does not validate this reaction's rate or selectivity. This sulfur-assisted branch involves addition and hydrolysis rather than the review's problematic reductive thione-to-nitrile exchange. FeS precipitation, H2S escape, and aminonitrile decomposition are explicit potential losses."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Conditional hydrolytic conversion of prolinethioamide to prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic geothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Proton-mediated thioamide hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: thioamide 0.1-10 mM, analytical acid concentration 0.01-0.5 M, and residence time 12 hours to 14 days. Transition from the sulfide-rich formation compartment to an acidic compartment is required. Measure amide, acid, residual thioamide, and sulfur-containing side products; H2S release does not imply quantitative environmental recycling."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to the activated thioamide and proton transfer permit replacement of sulfur by oxygen, giving prolinamide and hydrogen sulfide.", "reasoning": "Speculative: no substrate-specific primary support is supplied for this hydrolysis. Thioamide-to-amide conversion is chemically reasonable, but persistence of the thioamide, alternative cleavage, and direct progression to the acid must be tested. This step is the principal bottleneck of the sulfur-assisted workups. It requires no molecular oxygen and no assumed native-copper formation."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Exploratory carboxylative conversion of prolinonitrile to a bicyclic hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonia/ammonium acid-base buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar with a CO2-containing headspace", "catalyst": "General acid/base assistance by the aqueous buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: prolinonitrile 0.1-10 mM, total dissolved inorganic carbon 0.05-0.5 M, total ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M, measured pH 8.0-9.5, and residence time 1-14 days. Carbon supply, alkalinity, and CO2 exchange must be mutually compatible. The CO2 node represents the incorporated inorganic-carbon equivalent, not an assumption that all dissolved carbon is molecular CO2."}, "mechanism": "Proposed N-carboxylation of the secondary aminonitrile followed by intramolecular nitrile capture and rearrangement produces a fused hydantoin. The nitrile nitrogen becomes the second hydantoin nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Explicitly speculative and lower-confidence than direct hydrolysis. General hydantoin chemistry provides an analogy, as reviewed by Ware (1950), but neither that general analogy nor the supplied Patel et al. (2015) account establishes successful Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of this secondary cyclic aminonitrile under prebiotic conditions. Formation of the proposed bicyclic product must be demonstrated rather than inferred from atom balance. Competing reversible carbamate formation may prevent useful conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Exploratory opening of the bicyclic hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide-bearing geothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-mediated ring-opening hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: hydantoin 0.1-10 mM, analytical hydroxide concentration 0.01-0.2 M, and residence time 12 hours to 14 days. Verify which hydantoin C-N bond cleaves and whether N-carbamoylproline accumulates. Acid/base equivalents associated with product carboxylate formation must be supplied by the local reservoir."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the hydantoin amide bond adjacent to the proline-derived carbonyl opens the imidazolidinedione ring while preserving the pyrrolidine ring, producing N-carbamoylproline.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin opening to N-carbamoyl amino acids has general chemical precedent discussed by Ware (1950). The specific fused substrate, opening selectivity, and environmental rate are not established by the supplied research, so this implementation is speculative. General hydantoin precedent is not treated as reaction-specific primary evidence for this proposed proline branch."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Exploratory decarbamoylation to an L/D-proline mixture", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_002", "mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic aqueous reservoir supplied by volcanic HCl"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Proton-mediated urea hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: N-carbamoylproline 0.1-10 mM, analytical HCl concentration 0.5-2 M, and residence time 1-21 days. An acidic compartment is required after alkaline ring opening. Measure carbamoyl-group removal independently of substrate disappearance; allow for CO2 escape and ammonia protonation. No enzyme or nitrosating reagent is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis removes the N-carbamoyl group through carbamic-acid-equivalent intermediates, ultimately releasing carbon dioxide and ammonia and restoring the secondary amine of proline.", "reasoning": "Speculative: the supplied research does not establish chemical decarbamoylation of N-carbamoylproline under these conditions. Urea-bond hydrolysis is a plausible endpoint but may be too slow or require acidity incompatible with a persistent natural pool. This is an additional independent bottleneck of the hydantoin branch, not a demonstrated completion step. Achiral processing produces both L- and D-proline; no claim of enantiopure L-proline is made."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Aminoaldehyde entry with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Accept freshly available 4-aminobutanal as an explicit boundary input, cyclize, cyanate, and perform sequential acidic nitrile and amide hydrolysis. This is the most defensible conditional core, but substrate-specific kinetics, environmental acid availability, and integrated yield remain unverified. Produces an L-containing racemate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Aminoaldehyde entry with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Use the same ring-forming and cyanating chemistry as P1, followed by an alkaline hydrolysis regime. This is a distinct processing sequence, not a separately established carbon-skeleton synthesis. Requires persistent alkalinity and aminonitrile survival.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Aminoaldehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and acidic completion", "description": "Divert prolinonitrile through a proposed thioamide, hydrolyze that intermediate to prolinamide, and finish under acidic conditions. Thioamide formation and hydrolysis are unverified substrate-specific extensions; sulfur recycling is not assumed quantitative.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Aminoaldehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and alkaline completion", "description": "Follow the proposed sulfur-assisted route to prolinamide, then transfer to an alkaline compartment for the final hydrolysis. Both thioamide processing and the required acid-to-base environmental transition remain conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Aminoaldehyde entry with exploratory hydantoin processing", "description": "Propose carboxylative capture of prolinonitrile as a bicyclic hydantoin, followed by ring opening and decarbamoylation. This is a lower-confidence hypothesis with multiple unverified substrate-specific transformations, not a reported prebiotic proline synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Dialdehyde entry with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Prepend conditional reductive monoamination of succinaldehyde to the P1 sequence. Requires demonstrated formation and rapid release of 4-aminobutanal before overreduction, further amination, or condensation. No integrated yield is available.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Dialdehyde entry with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Combine the conditional hydrothermal precursor-supply branch with cyclic-imine cyanation and alkaline hydrolysis. The hydrogenation surface must be excluded from the cyanation compartment, and short-lived precursor delivery must be demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Dialdehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and acidic completion", "description": "Combine conditional dialdehyde monoamination with sulfur-assisted aminonitrile processing and acidic completion. Both precursor selectivity and thioamide hydrolysis are unresolved bottlenecks. Sulfide and cyanide must remain separated from the upstream hydrogenation surface.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Dialdehyde entry with sulfur-assisted hydration and alkaline completion", "description": "Use the dialdehyde precursor branch, proposed sulfur-assisted hydration, and an alkaline final hydrolysis. This sequence adds demanding catalyst-separation and pH-transition requirements to already unverified upstream and sulfur-processing reactions.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Dialdehyde entry with exploratory hydantoin processing", "description": "Connect conditional reductive monoamination to the exploratory hydantoin branch. This is the least-established complete sequence: precursor generation, bicyclic hydantoin formation, selective ring opening, and decarbamoylation all require direct testing. It is included as a transparent hypothesis, not as evidence that ten validated prebiotic routes exist.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_002", "mol_004", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_015", "mol_016"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_002", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_016", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 435723.27709198, 